---
import LessonLayout   from '@/layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import LessonHeader   from '@/components/LessonHeader.astro';
import LessonCallout  from '@/components/LessonCallout.astro';
import LessonDiagram  from '@/components/LessonDiagram.astro';
---

<LessonLayout title="2.3 — Weights, Embeddings & Attention: The Interview Panel">

  <LessonHeader
    num="2.3"
    title="Weights, Embeddings & Attention: The Interview Panel"
    subtitle="One scene, no math yet — everything from here builds on this exact picture."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      Before any formulas: one consistent real-world scene, and every core idea — weights,
      embeddings, attention, feedforward, layers — mapped onto it directly. The next lesson
      (2.4) covers the same ideas with real terminology and code. This one is just the picture.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Scene</h2>

    <p>
      You're one interviewer on a panel hiring for a Logistics Coordinator role. Between
      candidates, you need to answer: <strong>"Which candidate has actually handled a delayed
      shipment before?"</strong> You don't weigh every candidate's background equally — you
      unconsciously tune toward whoever's relevant and tune out the rest.
    </p>

    <div class="l0008-room">
      <div class="l0008-you">
        <span class="l0008-avatar">🎙️</span>
        <div class="l0008-bubble">"Which candidate has handled a delayed shipment before?"</div>
      </div>
      <div class="l0008-people">
        <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--high">
          <span class="l0008-avatar">🧍‍♀️</span>
          <div class="l0008-name">Sarah</div>
          <div class="l0008-sign">🏷️ shipment · delay · logistics</div>
        </div>
        <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--low">
          <span class="l0008-avatar">🧍</span>
          <div class="l0008-name">Marcus</div>
          <div class="l0008-sign">🏷️ event scheduling · sports</div>
        </div>
        <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--low">
          <span class="l0008-avatar">🧍‍♂️</span>
          <div class="l0008-name">Priya</div>
          <div class="l0008-sign">🏷️ hospitality · customer service</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="l0008-caption">Sarah's profile matches your question — she stands out. The others fade into the background.</p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Is the Question Being Asked to a Person, or a Function?</h2>

    <p>
      Worth settling before anything else: <strong>"You" in this scene is a function, not a
      person</strong> — a fixed, deterministic calculation that returns the same output every
      time it's given the same input. Every verb the rest of this lesson uses — listen, judge,
      think, decide — is a metaphor for one part of that calculation, not a claim that anything
      resembling a person is doing it.
    </p>

    <p>
      So the honest version of the opening question isn't "you're sitting across from candidates,
      deciding who's relevant." It's closer to: <strong>a fixed calculation gets handed some
      numbers, and returns other numbers, the same way every single time.</strong> The interview
      room, the panel, the listening — all of it is scaffolding to make that calculation
      graspable, not a claim that anything person-like is happening underneath it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">What Each Piece Represents</h2>

    <p>
      Every piece of the scene maps to a specific, real thing, not a vague vibe:
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>In the scene</th><th>In the model</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>"You," the interviewer asking a question</td><td>The computation for <em>one token position</em> — every token in the room is its own "You," running this at the same time.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Everyone in the interview room</td><td>The current context — every token in the conversation so far.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A candidate's profile</td><td>That token's <strong>Key</strong>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What they'd actually say if given the floor</td><td>That token's <strong>Value</strong>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Your question, right now</td><td>That token's <strong>Query</strong> — recomputed fresh, not fixed, at every layer (see "Layers" below).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The map of meaning</td><td>The <strong>embedding space</strong>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Your hiring instincts</td><td>The model's <strong>weights</strong>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A panel of interviewers, one after another</td><td>The stack of <strong>layers</strong> — each with its own, separately-trained weights.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Today's specific candidate pool</td><td><strong>Context</strong> — grows during one conversation, forgotten once it ends.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Your fundamental hiring judgment/skill</td><td><strong>Parameters</strong> — fixed until retrained, same for every candidate.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Embeddings — Meaning as Coordinates, Not Labels</h2>

    <p>
      For that "who's relevant" instinct to become something a computer can actually compute,
      every candidate first needs a precise <strong>coordinate</strong> — not a word tag, an
      actual point in space, decided by meaning. Candidates and backgrounds that are similar end
      up near each other on this map.
    </p>

    <div class="l0008-map">
      <span class="l0008-dot l0008-dot--person" style="left:64%;top:20%;" data-label="Sarah"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:58%;top:32%;" data-label="shipment"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:70%;top:38%;" data-label="logistics"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot l0008-dot--person" style="left:20%;top:58%;" data-label="Priya"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:16%;top:74%;" data-label="hospitality"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:30%;top:80%;" data-label="scheduling"></span>
    </div>
    <p class="l0008-caption">"Shipment" and "logistics" land close together. "Hospitality" lands far from both — different meaning, different neighborhood.</p>

    <p>
      Why coordinates specifically, not just labels? Three concrete reasons:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>You can calculate a match score.</strong> Two labels ("shipment" vs.
        "logistics") give a computer nothing to work with. Two coordinates let you do real
        arithmetic — multiply and add the numbers — and get back an actual match score you can
        rank candidates by, instead of a guess.</li>
      <li><strong>You can blend information.</strong> You can't "70%-blend" two sentences. You
        can take 70% of one coordinate and 30% of another, number by number, and get a new,
        meaningful coordinate back — this is exactly what happens once relevance is decided.</li>
      <li><strong>Relationships become measurable, not hand-written.</strong> Nobody tells the
        model "shipment and logistics are similar" — words used in similar contexts naturally end
        up at similar coordinates as a side effect of training. Because it's numeric, that
        closeness is something you can measure, not something a human pre-labeled.</li>
      </ul>

    <div class="l0008-math-note">
      <span class="l0008-math-tag">∑ in math terms</span>
      <p>An embedding is a lookup table: token id in, one fixed list of numbers out — e.g. token
        <code>4291</code> ("shipment") always maps to the same vector, something like
        <code>[0.12, -0.44, 0.91, …]</code>. That vector <em>is</em> the coordinate.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Attention — Query, Key, Value</h2>

    <p>
      Back on the panel, three things are happening at once:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Query</strong> — the question itself, in your head: <em>"who's handled a shipment delay?"</em></li>
      <li><strong>Key</strong> — what each candidate's profile signals about their background.</li>
      <li><strong>Value</strong> — the actual answer that candidate would give if given the floor.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      You compare your query against every candidate's key. High match → listen closely. Low
      match → mostly ignore. Then you blend together what everyone would say, weighted by how
      well their key matched — not "pick the one best candidate," a <em>blend</em>, weighted by
      relevance.
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  Q["❓ Query&colon; who's handled a<br/>shipment delay before?"]:::query
  K1["🏷️ Sarah's profile&colon;<br/>shipment, delay, logistics"]:::key
  K2["🏷️ Marcus's profile&colon;<br/>event scheduling, sports"]:::key
  K3["🏷️ Priya's profile&colon;<br/>hospitality, customer service"]:::key
  Q -->|"70% match"| K1
  Q -->|"5% match"| K2
  Q -->|"3% match"| K3
  K1 --> V1["💬 Sarah's answer"]:::value
  K2 --> V2["💬 Marcus's answer"]:::value
  K3 --> V3["💬 Priya's answer"]:::value
  V1 -->|"× 0.70"| OUT["🧠 Blended read&colon;<br/>mostly Sarah's answer, a little of the others"]:::out
  V2 -->|"× 0.05"| OUT
  V3 -->|"× 0.03"| OUT

  classDef query fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef key    fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef value  fill:#fef9c3,color:#713f12,stroke:#ca8a04,stroke-width:2px
  classDef out    fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <div class="l0008-math-note">
      <span class="l0008-math-tag">∑ in math terms</span>
      <p>Query, key, and value are each just the input coordinate multiplied by a different fixed
        matrix. "Match score" is a dot product (multiply the query and key numbers pairwise, add
        them up) — one number, higher when the two vectors point in a similar direction. The
        "blend" is a weighted sum of every value vector, using those match scores (turned into
        percentages via softmax) as the weights.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Weights — Where the Judgment Comes From</h2>

    <p>
      How do you even know how to read a candidate's profile and match it to your question? That
      skill isn't innate — years of interviewing taught you what "relevant experience" sounds
      like. <strong>Weights are that trained judgment, stored as numbers.</strong> Before any
      experience, your judgment is just random guessing — you'd rate Priya's hospitality
      background as highly as Sarah's logistics background, with no basis to tell them apart.
      Training is the slow process of correcting that judgment based on whether your hiring
      guesses turned out right.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Untrained ≠ broken, just random">
      <p>
        An untrained model has this exact same wiring — query, key, value, blending — fully
        functional. What it doesn't have is good judgment. Its weights are random numbers, so
        its "relevance scores" are meaningless, and its blended answer is noise. The
        architecture works from the start; the <em>judgment</em> only exists after training.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <div class="l0008-math-note">
      <span class="l0008-math-tag">∑ in math terms</span>
      <p>The "fixed matrices" mentioned above, for turning a coordinate into a query, a key, or a
        value — their actual numbers <em>are</em> the weights. Training doesn't change the
        formula (multiply by a matrix); it changes what numbers sit inside that matrix.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Fixed Judgment, Changing Candidate Pool</h2>

    <p>
      Your interviewing skill — how you read a profile, how you judge relevance — was built over
      years of past interviews, and it doesn't change during any given hiring round. To actually
      change that underlying judgment, you'd have to go conduct more interviews and let them
      reshape you. That's retraining. Nothing about one round updates your instincts.
    </p>

    <p>
      But <strong>who's currently in the candidate pool is a completely different thing</strong>
      — that's not your judgment, that's just who's in front of you today. You don't need new
      career experience to notice a candidate walked in and remember what they said five minutes
      ago. You're using your already-fixed skill to process an unfolding conversation. The skill
      is frozen; what it's currently looking at keeps changing.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="info" title="Parametric memory vs. context">
      <p>
        This split has a name: <strong>parametric memory</strong> (facts baked into the weights
        via training — slow, expensive, needs retraining to change) versus <strong>context</strong>
        (whoever's currently in the room — cheap, instant, gone once the session ends). Walk into
        tomorrow's hiring round and the pool is empty again — same trained skill, zero memory of
        yesterday's candidates, unless someone specifically hands you their file at the door.
        This is the entire idea behind RAG (retrieval-augmented generation): rather than
        retraining a model to "know" something new, you just make sure the relevant fact walks
        into the room as part of the context, and let the model's already-frozen judgment work
        with it.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Feedforward — The Private Thinking Step</h2>

    <p>
      After hearing a candidate's answer, you don't immediately score them — you take a beat,
      privately, to make sense of what you heard. Nobody else on the panel is involved yet; it's
      just you, alone, processing. Attention is "whose answer did I weigh." Feedforward is "what
      did I privately make of it." Every candidate's answer gets its own private processing,
      independently — and it's not a vague "mulling it over." It's a fixed, three-step routine:
    </p>

    <div class="l0008-advisors">
      <div class="l0008-advisor">
        <span class="l0008-avatar">🎧</span>
        <div class="l0008-adv-name">What you heard</div>
        <div class="l0008-adv-specialty">the blended impression<br/>from listening</div>
      </div>
      <div class="l0008-adv-arrow">→</div>
      <div class="l0008-advisor">
        <span class="l0008-avatar">🔎</span>
        <div class="l0008-adv-name">Run small checks</div>
        <div class="l0008-adv-specialty">"logistics-specific?" "composure<br/>under pressure?" — many at once</div>
      </div>
      <div class="l0008-adv-arrow">→</div>
      <div class="l0008-advisor">
        <span class="l0008-avatar">🚫</span>
        <div class="l0008-adv-name">Drop what didn't fire</div>
        <div class="l0008-adv-specialty">keep only the checks<br/>that actually matched</div>
      </div>
      <div class="l0008-adv-arrow">→</div>
      <div class="l0008-advisor">
        <span class="l0008-avatar">🧵</span>
        <div class="l0008-adv-name">Fold together</div>
        <div class="l0008-adv-specialty">the surviving checks become<br/>one refined understanding</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="l0008-caption">
      "Run small checks" and "drop what didn't fire" are the two fixed operations underneath —
      the same routine every single time, on whatever attention just handed over. Labels like
      "logistics-specific?" are illustrative: the real checks aren't human-nameable, but the
      shape — many small checks, keep the ones that fired, fold the rest away — is exactly right.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">What Is a Matrix, and Why Multiply by One?</h3>

    <p>
      A matrix is nothing more than a grid of numbers — rows across, columns down. Say attention
      just handed over the blended impression <code>[0.8, -0.2]</code> (shortened to two numbers
      here, for space — a real one has hundreds). "Run small checks" multiplies it by this grid:
    </p>

    <div class="l0008-matrix-grid">
      <div class="l0008-matrix-col-label">2 columns — one per input number</div>
      <div class="l0008-matrix-row">
        <table class="l0008-matrix-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th>col 1</th><th>col 2</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th>row 1</th><td>0.5</td><td>1.2</td></tr>
            <tr><th>row 2</th><td>-0.3</td><td>0.9</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="l0008-matrix-row-label">2 rows —<br/>one per<br/>output check</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      <strong>Columns must match the input</strong> — two numbers in, so two columns; column 1's
      weights always apply to input #1, column 2's to input #2. <strong>Rows are however many
      outputs you want</strong> — each row is its own complete, independent recipe. To compute
      one output, take one whole row, multiply it against the input position-by-position, and
      add:
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Row</th><th>Weighted sum</th><th>Result</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>row 1: <code>[0.5, 1.2]</code></td><td><code>0.5×0.8 + 1.2×(-0.2)</code></td><td><code>0.16</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>row 2: <code>[-0.3, 0.9]</code></td><td><code>-0.3×0.8 + 0.9×(-0.2)</code></td><td><code>-0.42</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      "Drop what didn't fire" is then the blunt rule from before: negative result → zero
      (<code>-0.42</code> gets dropped, <code>0.16</code> survives). Nothing here is a lookup, a
      guess, or a decision in any deliberative sense — it's the same fixed arithmetic, on
      whatever numbers came in.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">What a Matrix Is Actually Doing to the Coordinate</h3>

    <p>
      Here's the part worth being explicit about, since it's easy to see the arithmetic above and
      still not see why it matters: <strong>a matrix takes a point positioned in one coordinate
      space and re-expresses it in a different, purpose-built coordinate space.</strong> That's
      the actual connection back to the map of meaning from the Embeddings section.
    </p>

    <p>
      Sarah's embedding — her one general coordinate on that map — is a decent all-purpose
      summary of roughly what she's about, but it wasn't built for any one specific comparison.
      Recall from the Attention section that the same coordinate gets reshaped three different
      ways: into a <strong>Key</strong> (a version of her position built specifically for "how
      well do I match an incoming question"), a <strong>Value</strong> (a version built for "what
      do I actually have to contribute"), and — for whoever's asking — a <strong>Query</strong>
      (a version built for "what am I looking for"). Three different matrices, three different
      purpose-built reshapings of the exact same starting point. That's why the comparison in
      Attention (query vs. key) can be a simple, cheap calculation — dot the two together — even
      though that comparison wouldn't have meant anything done directly on the raw, general
      embeddings. The matrix did the work of moving both into a shared space where "closeness"
      actually means "relevance."</p>

    <p>
      Feedforward's matrix does the same kind of reshaping, just toward a different purpose: each
      row re-expresses the incoming coordinate as "how strongly does this look like the one
      specific pattern I check for" — many independent, purpose-built re-readings of the same
      point, computed in parallel.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Why a Weighted Sum, and Why It Needs a GPU</h3>

    <p>
      Weighted sums aren't the only way to combine numbers together — they're the choice that
      makes two other things possible, and the architecture is built around that trade-off, not
      around weighted sums being special in themselves. First, every weight is independently
      adjustable, which is exactly what training needs: a knob per weight that nudging up or
      down has a simple, predictable effect on the output. Messier operations mostly don't have
      that property, which makes them impractical to train at the scale this needs.
    </p>

    <p>
      Second — and this is why the zero-cutoff rule isn't optional decoration — stack weighted
      sums directly on top of each other with nothing in between, and no matter how many you
      stack, the result is mathematically no more powerful than a single one. Linear combinations
      of linear combinations are still just a linear combination. The cutoff is the one place a
      layer does something a pure weighted sum can't, and it's the reason adding interviewer 2 and
      interviewer 3 actually buys something interviewer 1 alone couldn't produce.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is also where the "checks" in the diagram above stop being just a teaching device and
      start explaining real hardware. Checking "logistics-specific?" doesn't require finishing
      "composure under pressure?" first — every row of that matrix is a completely independent
      calculation. A regular processor (a CPU) is built to do one complex task very fast, one
      after another — one interviewer working through the whole checklist alone. A GPU is built
      differently: thousands of small, simple cores, each capable of running one weighted sum at
      once, all simultaneously — the checklist handed out to a thousand assistants at the same
      moment instead of one interviewer working through it in order. Matrix multiplication is
      made entirely of identical, independent little calculations like this, which is exactly the
      shape of problem a GPU exists to solve — and exactly the shape of problem a CPU handles
      comparatively slowly, one weighted sum at a time.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="Is this actually &quot;thinking&quot;?">
      <p>
        Depends what the word means. If "thinking" means a deterministic mathematical
        transformation, shaped by training, that turns one set of numbers into a more useful set
        of numbers — yes, precisely that is happening. If "thinking" means what a person does —
        weighing options, being aware of considering something, being able to hesitate or change
        course mid-thought — none of that is happening here, and nothing in this operation could
        produce it. The calculation runs forward once, deterministically, and that's the whole
        event. There's no "inside" where deliberation could occur — just the weighted sums above.
      </p>
      <p>
        The word gets used because the <em>output</em> resembles what thinking produces (a
        rough impression getting refined into a sharper one) — not because the
        <em>mechanism</em> resembles how a person thinks. Worth keeping those two senses of the
        word apart every time it shows up in this lesson.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <div class="l0008-math-note">
      <span class="l0008-math-tag">∑ in math terms</span>
      <p>Feedforward = multiply by a matrix (expand), zero out negatives, multiply by a second
        matrix (compress back down). Two matrix multiplications and one comparison. That's the
        entire operation this section has been calling "private thinking."</p>
    </div>

    <LessonCallout variant="info" title="&quot;Feedforward&quot; means two different things">
      <p>
        The private-thinking routine above is the narrow meaning. The word is also used more
        broadly for the whole architecture: information flows only one direction — interviewer 1
        to interviewer 2 to interviewer 3, never backward — which makes the entire chain "a
        feedforward network," as opposed to designs where information can loop back on itself.
        Same word, two scopes: one sub-step inside a layer, or the whole one-directional chain of
        layers. Everywhere else in this lesson, "feedforward" means the narrow one.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Layers — The Interview Panel, Not One Person Trying Again</h2>

    <p>
      A layer is <em>not</em> you repeating the same listen-then-think cycle with your own
      judgment. It's handing your current notes to a <strong>different interviewer on the panel
      — someone with their own, separately trained judgment</strong> — who runs their own
      listen-and-think cycle starting from what you handed them, then passes their refined notes
      to the next interviewer in line. Nobody designs what each interviewer is good at; it
      emerges from training. In practice, earlier interviewers tend to catch surface-level
      patterns, later ones more abstract relationships.
    </p>

    <p>
      What actually gets passed at each hand-off: <strong>one refined coordinate per candidate in
      the room</strong>, not one value for the whole scene. Every candidate gets their own
      updated coordinate from every interviewer — Sarah's, Marcus's, Priya's — all produced in
      that same round, all the same size as what came in, ready to be handed to the next
      interviewer.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Each interviewer isn't re-asking your original question — they're asking their own,
      sharper question</strong>, made possible by what the previous interviewer already figured
      out. The query gets recomputed fresh at every layer, from whatever the current
      understanding is — not replayed unchanged from layer to layer.
    </p>

    <div class="l0008-advisors">
      <div class="l0008-advisor">
        <span class="l0008-avatar">🧑‍🦱</span>
        <div class="l0008-adv-name">Interviewer 1</div>
        <div class="l0008-adv-specialty">🔍 catches the topic</div>
        <div class="l0008-adv-question">"who mentioned a shipment delay?"</div>
      </div>
      <div class="l0008-adv-arrow">→</div>
      <div class="l0008-advisor">
        <span class="l0008-avatar">🧑‍🦰</span>
        <div class="l0008-adv-name">Interviewer 2</div>
        <div class="l0008-adv-specialty">🧩 connects the pieces</div>
        <div class="l0008-adv-question">"did Sarah resolve it herself?"</div>
      </div>
      <div class="l0008-adv-arrow">→</div>
      <div class="l0008-advisor">
        <span class="l0008-avatar">🧑</span>
        <div class="l0008-adv-name">Interviewer 3</div>
        <div class="l0008-adv-specialty">💡 reads between the lines</div>
        <div class="l0008-adv-question">"how did she handle the pressure?"</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="l0008-caption">
      Each interviewer = one layer, its own trained weights. The question sharpens at every
      hand-off — interviewer 2 couldn't have asked "did Sarah resolve it herself" without
      already knowing, from interviewer 1, that a shipment delay is what's being discussed.
    </p>

    <div class="l0008-math-note">
      <span class="l0008-math-tag">∑ in math terms</span>
      <p>A layer = one attention step + one feedforward step, using that layer's own matrices.
        "Interviewer 2" is just that same pair of operations run again, with a different set of
        learned numbers, on the output of interviewer 1.</p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">How the Panel's View of the Room Changes</h3>

    <p>
      There's a second thing evolving alongside the sharpening question. Each interviewer doesn't
      just inherit the previous interviewer's notes and think them over — they also
      <strong>re-read the whole room fresh</strong>, through their own personal lens (their own
      trained Query/Key/Value matrices), before folding anything in. The same three candidates
      get looked at again and again, and each interviewer notices something a little different.
    </p>

    <div class="l0008-multiview">
      <div class="l0008-view">
        <div class="l0008-view-label">Interviewer 1's read</div>
        <div class="l0008-view-people">
          <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--high"><div class="l0008-name">Sarah</div><div class="l0008-sign">mentions shipping</div></div>
          <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--low"><div class="l0008-name">Marcus</div><div class="l0008-sign">mentions scheduling</div></div>
          <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--low"><div class="l0008-name">Priya</div><div class="l0008-sign">mentions service</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="l0008-view-arrow">→</div>
      <div class="l0008-view">
        <div class="l0008-view-label">Interviewer 2's read</div>
        <div class="l0008-view-people">
          <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--high"><div class="l0008-name">Sarah</div><div class="l0008-sign">handled a shipment delay</div></div>
          <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--low"><div class="l0008-name">Marcus</div><div class="l0008-sign">scheduling ≠ logistics</div></div>
          <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--low"><div class="l0008-name">Priya</div><div class="l0008-sign">no logistics background</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="l0008-view-arrow">→</div>
      <div class="l0008-view">
        <div class="l0008-view-label">Interviewer 3's read</div>
        <div class="l0008-view-people">
          <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--high"><div class="l0008-name">Sarah</div><div class="l0008-sign">resolved it calmly — strong fit</div></div>
          <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--faded"><div class="l0008-name">Marcus</div></div>
          <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--faded"><div class="l0008-name">Priya</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="l0008-caption">
      Same candidates throughout — nobody left the room. What changed is how each interviewer's
      own matrices reshape their profiles into relevance for that interviewer's specific
      question. By interviewer 3, Marcus and Priya have all but faded from consideration — not
      because they left, but because interviewer 3's lens finds almost nothing in their profiles
      relevant to the question at hand.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Watching the Coordinate Narrow Down</h3>

    <p>
      Each hand-off doesn't just sharpen the question — it moves the <strong>coordinate itself</strong>
      on the map of meaning from the Embeddings section, and shrinks how uncertain it is. Same
      map, same "shipment"/"logistics" neighborhood — now tracking where the current
      understanding actually sits after each interviewer.
    </p>

    <div class="l0008-map">
      <span class="l0008-dot l0008-dot--person" style="left:64%;top:20%;" data-label="Sarah"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:58%;top:32%;" data-label="shipment"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:70%;top:38%;" data-label="logistics"></span>

      <svg class="l0008-path-svg" viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none">
        <polyline points="38,62 58,35 66,23" class="l0008-path-line" />
        <circle cx="38" cy="62" r="17" class="l0008-path-circle" />
        <circle cx="58" cy="35" r="9" class="l0008-path-circle" />
        <circle cx="66" cy="23" r="3" class="l0008-path-circle" />
      </svg>

      <span class="l0008-path-label" style="left:38%;top:62%;">after interviewer 1</span>
      <span class="l0008-path-label" style="left:58%;top:35%;">after interviewer 2</span>
      <span class="l0008-path-label" style="left:66%;top:23%;">after interviewer 3</span>
    </div>
    <p class="l0008-caption">
      The large, fuzzy circle after interviewer 1 means "broadly somewhere around this topic, not
      committed to specifics." By interviewer 3, the circle has tightened almost onto Sarah's own
      coordinate — the understanding has narrowed from "something shipment-related" down to
      something precise enough to actually answer the question with.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">What If an Interviewer Is Bad?</h3>

    <p>
      An interviewer's judgment can be bad two ways: undertrained (their instincts are still
      close to random — see "Untrained ≠ broken, just random" above), or just unhelpful for this
      particular candidate. Either way, the damage is bounded, because of one detail the scene
      has glossed over: <strong>an interviewer doesn't replace your notes with their own take —
      they add their refinement on top of them.</strong>
    </p>

    <div class="l0008-advisors">
      <div class="l0008-advisor">
        <span class="l0008-avatar">🗒️</span>
        <div class="l0008-adv-name">What you had</div>
        <div class="l0008-adv-specialty">your notes<br/>before this interviewer</div>
      </div>
      <div class="l0008-adv-arrow">+</div>
      <div class="l0008-advisor">
        <span class="l0008-avatar">🧑‍🦱</span>
        <div class="l0008-adv-name">Their contribution</div>
        <div class="l0008-adv-specialty">could be sharp,<br/>could be noise</div>
      </div>
      <div class="l0008-adv-arrow">=</div>
      <div class="l0008-advisor">
        <span class="l0008-avatar">📝</span>
        <div class="l0008-adv-name">What the next interviewer gets</div>
        <div class="l0008-adv-specialty">your original notes,<br/>still intact, plus their addition</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="l0008-caption">
      A confused interviewer dents the assessment a little — their noise gets added in. They
      never get to erase what you already knew. This is a real, named part of the architecture
      (a "residual connection"), and it's the reason stacking dozens of interviewers doesn't mean
      dozens of chances for one bad one to wreck everything.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Candidate Pool Isn't Static</h2>

    <p>
      The room isn't a snapshot — two things about it keep changing.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Candidates keep entering.</strong> Every new token is a new candidate walking
      through the door. Once someone's been interviewed, their notes stay on file — but a
      candidate who just walked in can be assessed in light of everyone already interviewed
      today, while nobody already assessed ever gets reconsidered based on who walks in later.
      Their read was formed before the newcomer arrived, and it's locked in. That rule is called
      <strong>causal masking</strong>, and it's why generation has to happen one token at a time,
      in order — nobody in the room is allowed to know who's about to walk in next. The room also
      has a capacity — the <strong>context window</strong> — and letting candidates in forever
      eventually means either turning people away at the door or the room getting too crowded to
      track everyone in it well.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>The same name can mean different people.</strong> Imagine two different candidates
      named Sarah interviewing on the same day. Glance at name tags alone and they're identical —
      that name tag is the <strong>static embedding</strong>, the same starting coordinate every
      time, regardless of which Sarah or which sentence. But as each Sarah actually answers
      questions — discusses different backgrounds, gets folded into different lines of
      questioning — her representation gets refined, layer after layer, by what's actually been
      discussed with her. By the third interviewer, "Sarah who worked in shipping" and "Sarah who
      worked in retail" have drifted into genuinely different coordinates, despite starting at
      the exact same spot. That's a <strong>contextual embedding</strong> — the same word
      reshaped differently every time by whatever surrounds it, which is a big part of why this
      architecture beats older one-fixed-vector-per-word methods.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Beam — Not Committing Too Early</h2>

    <p>
      If it's unclear yet whether Sarah's most relevant experience is "the shipment delay" or
      "the warehouse audit" — both got mentioned — instead of committing to one guess
      immediately, keep both possibilities in mind as you keep listening, and let more
      information settle which one actually pans out. Lesson 2.5 walks through this live, on the
      running <code>17 × 24</code> example.
    </p>

  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Go Further</h2>
